	url = {https://pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.1222099110},
	doi = {10.1073/pnas.1222099110},
	abstract = {{SignificanceMicrobial} communities regulate the cycling of energy and matter in the environment, yet how they respond to environmental change is not well-known. We describe here a day in the life of wild planktonic microbial species using robotic sampling coupled with genome-wide gene expression analysis. Our results showed that closely related populations, as well as very different bacterial and archaeal species, displayed remarkably similar time-variable synchronous patterns of gene expression over 2 d. Our results suggest that specific environmental cues may elicit cross-species coordination of gene expression among diverse microbial groups, potentially enabling multispecies coupling of metabolic activity.},
